From 890e953cdf0e7dab694bdca7b97670bd20f63ed3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: batya <batya@github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 01:57:56 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] feat(studio): a control strip with a message log, sessions of
 their own, and lists that read a page

The window had no way to say where a track came from, and no place to say what an agent did.
This adds both: a ToolStrip above the list, a message log behind the bell, and sessions that live
in the service instead of the window's localStorage. New pieces are components of their own; the
author's files carry small, commented seams and none of his behaviour is rewritten.

Sessions live in library.sqlite (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, as the rest of the schema asks): one
is open at a time, the active one stands first in the list, opening one from the library offers to
switch to editing, and the routes for generation, stems, karaoke, processing, MIDI and import take
an explicit workspace_id instead of trusting whatever happens to be open. A track made outside any
session is gathered once into a session the studio keeps for itself, marked kind='import' in
session_kinds - a mark in the data, not a name, so two windows can no longer end up with two
sessions named in the same language and holding different tracks. The repeat mode of a context is
stored in the library too.

The makers take a session word - each (a session of its own for every track, named after the
track), current, or new:<name> - and without a word the window asks the person once for the whole
pack; the answer can be remembered for the work or for good. A track is bound to the session that
was named, not to the active one, and workspace_get says where it really landed. The makers refuse
to run while nothing is open and nobody has chosen, so no track is ever made behind a person's
back.

The message log says what happened in the person's own language: the service sends facts - the
action, the kind of thing, and that thing's own name, read from the library for a deletion -
rather than a tool's name, and the window builds the sentence in all five languages. Every change
leaves a line quietly, the window's or an agent's; reads do not. Clearing the log asks first.

The agent's leash (free, risky, all) and its remembered answers live in the settings rather than
in the agent's hands: "in this session" is kept with the session itself, "always" is one switch,
"never" is a list of forbidden tools, and a deletion is put to the person as one plain question.
The skill the server serves now describes sessions, the leash and the stems archive for a
connecting agent.

Lists read a page at a time and sort by created, updated or name; a stem is shown inside the song
it came from and is never counted as a song; the like button has tooltips in the card and in all
four player layouts, and every string a person reads lives in all five languages.

Gates: tsc --noEmit, vitest (35) and cargo test -p music-server --lib (232) pass; preflight
reports READY for a pull request.
